21:59:52 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a brilliant analogy, and it gets right to the tension. A public library wouldn't hand you a book with paragraphs blacked out, so why does the government? The key is that **a library book and a government record are fundamentally different kinds of things**, created for entirely different purposes. You're comparing a published product to a raw internal file. Here's why the library analogy breaks down, and why that breakdown explains the redactions: ### 1. A Library Book Is a Finished Product for the Public Every book on a library shelf was intentionally written, edited, and published for public consumption. By the time you touch it, the author and publisher have already decided everything in it is safe and appropriate for you to read. The library's job is to give you that finished product as is. There's no hidden, sensitive information in a novel that needs to be covered because it was never there to begin with. ### 2. A Government Record Is a Raw Byproduct of Work FOIA requests don't ask for glossy, published government brochures (those you can usually just download). They ask for the raw materials of government: internal emails, investigative reports, strategy memos, personnel files, intelligence briefs. These documents weren't written for you. They were written to get a job done—catch a criminal, advise a cabinet secretary, manage an employee. In the process of doing that work, the documents inadvertently captured things that aren't meant for public eyes: - A crime witness's name and address. - A general's candid opinion that a foreign leader is unstable. - An informant's phone number. - An employee's disciplinary record. The document is just the container. Redaction is the process of saying: "We want to give you the container and most of its contents because you have a right to see what we're doing, but we have to lock the compartment with the private medical records because that's not yours to see." ### 3. The Library Does Have Restricted Materials (You Just Don't See Them) Ironically, libraries *do* have materials that aren't fully open. Rare book archives often have strict rules—you might need supervision, no photocopies, no flash photography. Librarians don't hand out their internal circulation records showing what books you've checked out; that's considered your private information. The difference is that those records aren't on the open shelf, just like many exempt government records aren't handed out. But when a FOIA request includes a document that's *mostly* open but has a sliver of private information, they can't just hide the whole file in a back room. They bring it to you with a black bar over the private part—it's the compromise between total denial and total exposure. ### 4. You'd Want Your Own Information Protected Picture this: you're the subject of a criminal investigation that got dropped. A FOIA request for the case files comes in. Would you want the library to hand over a complete, unredacted book containing your accusations, your statements, and the names of your family members—all unproven? Or would you want the sensitive, personal parts blacked out before it's released to the public? The very "freedom" you're defending would destroy your own privacy if it were absolute. So, the library analogy works perfectly *if* you think of FOIA not as checking out a published book, but as asking for the librarian's personal, back-office planning notes—including the parts that mention private patron data and security codes. You wouldn't expect them to hand you that notebook intact; you'd expect them to rip out the private pages first. FOIA redactions are that ripping-out process, done carefully so you still get the rest.
