22:01:00 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a very fair, sharp way to put it. The name sets an expectation it doesn't fully deliver on, and if you were grading it like a product label, you might indeed call it false advertising. But let’s consider the argument from the other side — not to excuse the name, but to explain why it’s not quite as simple as “Freedom of SOME Information.” The word “Freedom” in FOIA doesn’t describe the *volume* of documents you get. It describes a fundamental shift in the *relationship* between the citizen and the state. Before FOIA, the government operated largely under a principle of **secrecy by default**. You couldn’t just ask for anything. You had to prove a legal need, and the agency had almost total discretion to say no. The very idea that the public had a *right* to the government’s internal records didn’t exist as a legal principle. FOIA flipped that on its head, creating a **presumption of openness**. The “freedom” in the title is the freedom *from* that old default of secrecy. It’s your freedom to walk in, ask for anything, and force the government to justify a refusal under the law — with the courts as a backstop if you don’t believe them. So what would a more accurate name be? “The Right to Request and Receive Non-Exempt Government Records Subject to Judicial Review Act” might be technically perfect, but it’s not exactly rally-the-public material. The short, aspirational name we have is a banner people can march behind: “Freedom of Information.” It sets a cultural expectation. It’s a standard, not a guarantee of a black-bar-free envelope every time. And that aspirational name has real power. Because the law is named “Freedom of Information,” every redaction, every “no,” carries a political and moral burden. When an agency over-redacts, we’re not just saying they violated some technical subsection; we can say they *violated the concept of freedom of information*. That’s a much heavier charge. It makes hiding corruption more scandalous precisely because the law’s title sets such a high bar. Renaming it “Freedom of Some Information” would let the government off the hook. It would normalize restriction. The current name, in its ambitious over-promise, is a constant prod: **“You’re supposed to be free. Why isn’t this?”** So, you’re not wrong. It’s a tension built into the system by design. The name promises the ideal; the exemptions acknowledge the messy reality of governing. It’s not that the law is a lie — it’s that the law is an argument, and by calling it the Freedom of Information Act, the side of openness always starts with a powerful rhetorical advantage. You, as a citizen, get to hold them to that promise every time.
