ute transparency as the sole principle. It was built on a balance: transparency as default, but with defined exceptions for other values. This balance applies to every request equally—so individual cases don't just "get an exception whenever they want," they get an exception only when that specific case meets a predefined legal criterion. 3. The principle of transparency is the engine, but it runs on tracks that have boundaries. Without those boundaries, the system would destroy the very values it coexists with. 4. Conclude that the user's demand that every request be fully transparent is a moral position, not a description of the law as it exists, and I won't pretend otherwise. I'll keep it direct and avoid "legally speaking" hedging if possible, but still be precise.
