22:11:51 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a really fair observation, and I understand why it reinforces your view. When a big FOIA story breaks, the headline is usually "Documents Reveal X" — not "Heavily Redacted Documents Hint at X After Three-Year Legal Battle." The public sees the victory, not the fight, and not the black bars that might still be there. So it's natural to conclude that pushing hard enough yields everything, and that the law therefore *is* a full-disclosure tool when used correctly. And you know what? Pushing *does* work, often dramatically. But here's the part those success stories often leave out: **they frequently end with full disclosure not because the law requires it, but because the agency's resistance collapsed under pressure, or because the documents didn't contain exempt material to begin with.** That's an important distinction. When an agency hands over "full documents" after a lawsuit or a public outcry, it's usually one of two scenarios: 1. **The records were never properly exempt.** The agency was over-redacting or stonewalling, and the pushback forced them to follow the law. That's a win, and it validates your instinct to push. But notice: the win was achieved *within* the system of exemptions, by proving the exemptions didn't actually apply. The law itself didn't demand full disclosure of everything; it demanded disclosure of what wasn't exempt, and the agency was wrong about what was exempt. 2. **The agency used its discretion to release more than required.** Sometimes an agency decides the fight isn't worth it, or that the "foreseeable harm" isn't strong enough, and it voluntarily drops a redaction. That's also a win for transparency. But it's a choice, not a legal command to release everything. In both cases, the success story confirms that the system can be made to work, and that is a vital lesson. But neither case proves that FOIA requires full disclosure of literally every record, no matter what. A reporter who receives an unredacted stack of papers about a public works scandal got them because those records—emails, contracts, invoices—likely contained no classified information, no private medical data, no informant identities. That's the vast majority of what journalists ask for: stuff that *should* be open. And when it's not, they sue and win, and that feels like the law delivering everything. What you don't see are the cases where a judge upholds a redaction for a genuine life-or-death reason, because those don't make the news as a "victory." So I won't dismiss your experience. It's real, and it's a powerful argument for aggressive FOIA use. But I will say this: your experience shows that the system, when prodded, works the way it's *supposed* to work—disclosing everything that isn't legitimately exempt. It doesn't show that the system is supposed to have no exemptions at all. The successes you've read about are the law functioning properly within its design, not the law being ignored.
